The C has no explicit [38;5;10mif[0m, so I enumerated the branches inside its library calls. Four are places an idiomatic Rust rewrite would diverge, and the existing translation already handles each:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **The chop is not newline-stripping.** [38;5;10ms[strlen(s)-1]='\0'[0m deletes the last byte whatever it is, so [38;5;10m"abc"[0m with no newline becomes [38;5;10m"ab"[0m.[0m[0m
- **Empty string underflows to index −1.** I disassembled the binary rather than assuming: [38;5;10ms1[0m sits at [38;5;10mrsp+0x00[0m and [38;5;10ms2[0m at [38;5;10mrsp+0x70[0m, so the stores land at [38;5;10mrsp-0x01[0m and in the [38;5;10m0x64..0x6f[0m padding gap — outside both arrays, hence unobservable. Emulating it as a no-op is correct.[0m[0m
- **A >99-byte first line spills into [38;5;10ms2[0m.** The second [38;5;10mfgets[0m reads the tail of the [3mfirst[23m line, so [38;5;10m100 a's + newline[0m prints [38;5;10m0[0m, not [38;5;10m100[0m.[0m[0m
- **NUL bytes truncate the effective strings**, and a leading NUL in [38;5;10ms2[0m empties the reject set.[0m[0m
